d you're eventually  going to get this money back and I  remember I had a conversation with my  grandparents which I'm sure a lot of  young people have about Social Security  and he told me I've paid into Social  Security my whole career I paid into  Medicare everything I'm getting now I  deserve it it was my money but instead  what happened is politicians have sold a  false bill they've said all these  programs that you're paying for now  you're gonna get the money you paid in  later but they've made promises where  they give benefit  now and push the cost off to the future  which means that current retirees are  getting far more back than what they  paid into the system so were you having  that conversation with your grandfather  as you were like pushing the pillow on  his face yeah well well it's interesting  because a lot of times you want to frame  this as a generational warfare issue but  I think it really isn't it's more of  Washington versus America's young not  boomers and the Silent Generation about  you know Medicare and Social Security I  think it's in your book you mentioned  it's basically about 40% or four out of  every ten dollars that spent are in  those programs and that percentage is  only going to go up you know what should  be done to Medicare and Social Security  well Social Security is an easier fix  all we need to do is realize that we  live in a different age now people live  longer they're more wealthy when they're  older so what we need to do is increase  the retirement age and also index  benefits to actual costs of living  rather than wages and so just simple  changes around the edges can bring  Social Security into balance and long  term Medicare is much scarier but it's a  difficult to predict because there could  be so much innovation and medicine that  costs may go down if we found let's say  a way to alleviate some forms of cancer  but right now the current traductor  trajectory it's on I would say Medicare  is a much scarier problem in an ideal  world at least we're in a libertarian  world wouldn't we get rid of these  programs and and not simply to say that  you know people should just fend for  themselves but Medicare was predicated  you know as much as Social Security on  the idea of old people who didn't have  insurance and would just die horrible  painful deaths that's not really the  case anymore many people are wealthy and  can afford their own insurance so should  we shift from in h bay if we're gonna  have a government provided social safety  net should we switch from an age base  one to a needs-based one would that be a  part I think that makes sense and also  just introducing more choice especially  in Medicare there's no reason you should  have to be kicked off your private  insurance once you turn 65 just to go on  Medicaid although legally you must  enroll in Medicare yeah so this doesn't  make any sense  and it's because they want to keep up  the facade that these are programs that  are social insurance we're all paying  for it but really starting to means-test  these programs is something we need to  do and  make it match the reality which is that  there are tax and transfer programs  where the young people are being taxed  and their Wells being transferred to  older Americans part two is keeping  young people uneducated and I'll speak  slowly because I realize you're younger  than I am  and you talk about the failure of K  through 12 education as well as the  explosion in college debt is is it true  that education is worse now than it was  say 30 or 40 years ago the stuff that I  see mostly is that results are about the  same but you know it's just that the  cost has gone up two or three percent  two or three times well I'd say American  students they have stayed about the same  but the rest of the world has shot ahead  of us so while the rest of the world is  adapting and improving their education  system the u.s. education system is  stagnating but this is one of the areas  where I'm the most optimistic we've seen  Nevada passing pretty much  individualized funding for students that  then they can save we've seen in a lot  of urban areas the growth at charter  schools and this is one area that I  think that debates pretty much won and  now we're gonna see more and more school  choice and I mean the Manhattan  Institute I know is is very free market  oriented and interested in the ways in  which markets are really discovery  processes I you know one of the things  that always struck me as odd is when in  an industry as vast as K through 12  education when you have everybody  basically doing the same thing you know  some kind of weird government and force  monopolies going on and that might end  with individualized learning right yeah  it could definitely chip away at it and  also chip away at the power of the  teachers unions and what I've seen cuz  we talk to a lot of teachers a lot of  students in the book and we discovered  that the teachers unions aren't even  standing up for teachers interests  instead what they're doing is looking  out for union bosses and current  retirees and they're making really a  system that goes against young teachers  as well as students well we'll leave it  there we've been talking with Jared  Meyer he's the co-author with the  Manhattan Institute's Diane  furchtgott-roth of disinherited how  Washington is betraying America's young  Jared thanks for talking to us hey  thanks for having me for reason TV I'm  Nick Gillespie

